$DIR/main.p:19:34: parameter limit has no default value but follows parameter factor, which has one
    A call may leave out only trailing parameters. So once a parameter has a
    default value, every parameter after it needs one too.
    (see "Default parameter values" in the Plutonium spec, section 10.13)
    (run "pluto explain E2203" for a longer explanation)

$DIR/main.p:12:2: missing cases in switch on c (Color): Green, Blue (add them or a default case)
    A switch on an enum value that has no default case must list every member.
    That way, adding a member to the enum shows every switch that needs to
    handle it.
    Add a case for each missing member, or a default case if the remaining
    members share one action.
    Go does not check this. Plutonium does, because an enum is a closed set of
    values, so the compiler can tell when one is forgotten.
    (see "Switch statements" in the Plutonium spec, section 12.9)
    (run "pluto explain E2312" for a longer explanation)

$DIR/main.p:21:50: errors.New needs a constant message, since each call site has its own error type, made when compiling; use fmt.Errorf to build a message at run time
    errors.New takes only a constant message, such as a string literal. The
    compiler makes an error type for each place errors.New is called, with a
    counter in the program image, so the message must be known when compiling.
    To build a message from values at run time, use fmt.Errorf, which allocates
    the error.
    In Go, errors.New allocates a new error on every call. Plutonium never
    allocates behind the program's back, so each evaluation of errors.New is a
    new error made from the call site's type and a counter, which costs one
    atomic addition and needs no allocator.
    To include values in the message:
        return fmt.Errorf("open %s: not found", name)
    (see "Sentinels and errors.New" in the Plutonium spec, section 16.3)
    (run "pluto explain E2008" for a longer explanation)

$DIR/main.p:26:22: map element type file is an owner, which a map cannot hold: reading the map copies its elements
    An owner owns memory or another resource and releases it when it is dropped,
    so it moves instead of being copied. A map copies its keys and elements as
    Go's does: v := m[k] and range give copies, and m2 := m shares the table.
    Two copies of an owner would both release what it owns, so a map cannot hold
    one.
    Go's garbage collector frees what a map's values refer to. Plutonium frees
    memory when its owner is dropped, so a map, whose elements are copied
    freely, can hold values and counted references but not owners.
    Store a counted reference to the value instead, which copies by counting and
    releases the value with its last copy:
        m := map[string]synced[file]{}
    (see "Map types" in the Plutonium spec, section 7.10)
    (run "pluto explain E3213" for a longer explanation)
